      "chapterNumber": 5,
      "headline": "Step 1: Listen Empathically: Tuning Into the Right Frequency",
      "keyInsight": "We love to believe we are world-class multitaskers who can digest someone's heartbreak while clearing an inbox. That is pure fantasy. True listening is an active physical act, not passive background noise. The bizarre truth is that when you plan your brilliant response while someone talks, you are not listening at all. You are just reloading your conversational weapon. Human beings possess built-in radar for faked attention. We sense eye shifts, stiff postures, and absent nods instantly. When your body is in the room but your brain is elsewhere, the speaker feels like they are talking to a mannequin. Empathic listening demands that you set down your mental toolbox and simply witness the weather. You step out into the rain with them instead of critiquing their lack of an umbrella.",
      "content": "Imagine tuning an old analog radio on a cross-country drive. When you sit halfway between two stations, you get nothing but harsh static and squeals. You have to roll the dial slowly until the voice rings clear. Empathic listening is adjusting your internal dial to their exact frequency. If your mind buzzes with to-do lists, you only hear static.\n\nMost listeners make the mistake of acting like a defense lawyer. They spot factual errors in real time. They interrupt to fix dates, names, or minor details. But an upset person is not giving testimony under oath. They are describing how the world feels inside their chest right now. Correcting small facts during an emotional storm is like polishing hubcaps while the car rolls down a hill.\n\nPut away the glowing phone screen. Turn your torso toward the person. Keep your mouth shut and your eyes curious. You are not listening to judge, grade, or correct their story. You are listening to understand why their engine is rattling.",
