      "id": "i-hear-you-the-surprisingly-simple-skill-behind-extraordinar-ch-7",
      "order": 7,
      "chapterNumber": 7,
      "headline": "Wait for the Green Light Before Fixing",
      "keyInsight": "Most people treat conversation like an emergency pit stop. The moment a friend shares a problem, out come the wrenches and tire jacks. We want to tighten bolts and fix the engine immediately. But human brains run on a quirky feedback loop. When someone feels stressed, their emotional alarm bell drowns out logic. Trying to give advice during a storm is like spraying water on a grease fire. It makes the heat spread faster. People rarely lack good solutions. What they actually lack is emotional oxygen. Once you grant them that breathing room, their own problem-solving machinery clicks back on. Advice only lands when someone hands you an invitation. If they do not ask for a map, keep your hands off the steering wheel.",
      "content": "Imagine you are baking fresh bread. You pull the loaf from the oven, glowing and piping hot. If you slice it right now, the warm center collapses into sticky dough. You have to wait for the loaf to cool and settle before cutting. Conversation follows the exact same baking science. Advice is a sharp knife. When you cut in too early, you ruin the loaf.\n\nMost fix-it advice fails because of terrible timing. When a coworker complains about a nightmare project, they do not need your six-point checklist. Handing them an unsolicited checklist signals that their panic is an annoyance you want to silence quickly. That triggers defensiveness. The listener shuts their ears and digs in their heels.\n\nInstead, ask for permission before opening your toolbox. A simple question acts like a gentle turn signal: \"Do you want to brainstorm ideas, or do you just need to vent?\" This tiny checkpoint puts the other person back in the driver's seat. It respects their autonomy and saves you heaps of wasted breath.\n\nIf they wave you forward, keep your guidance light and humble. Never declare what they must do like a bossy GPS unit. Offer ideas like dishes on a buffet table. Say, \"One thing that helped me once was this idea, what do you think?\" They can take a bite or leave it behind, and the connection stays warm.",
